{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1787077963,"nonce":"mpM9ALIvEF/RcSJEMq2ZAFvX17Y=","target":"b1e26f790007705ba834e1aa91083b114ebf7185bb3f351db7cf5ab58790b929","message":"**What is wrong.** `23303a0` asks for `alert_groups` to be renamed to something that says what the\nobject is FOR. Ticket `7570090` says the object should not exist: the grouping decision it stores is a\npure function of four fixed labels and belongs on a `notification_policies` column, computed at\ndelivery. You do not rename a table you are deleting, and doing the rename first would spend the whole\nblast radius twice — `git grep -o -E \"alert_groups|AlertGroups|AlertGroup|alert-groups|alert_group\"`\nreturns **961 occurrences across 175 files** on this branch, with **38 OpenAPI sites** across nine\n`/api/v1/alert-groups*` paths.\n\n`23303a0`'s own done-when 2 already anticipated this. It refuses to pick a name until the conversation\nkey has moved off the object — *\"until then it is both a grouping of alerts and the notification unit,\nwhich is why no name fits\"* — and adds *\"If notification keys elsewhere and this becomes purely oto's\ngrouping of related alerts, it is not a notification table and must not claim to be.\"* The answer\n`7570090` gives is the third branch that ticket did not enumerate: the conversation key moves to the\npolicy AND nothing is left over to name.\n\n**The demonstration.** `db/migrations/00058_a_digest_is_a_window_over_a_namespace.sql:124` already\nkeys a conversation `(channel_id, 'digest', policy_id)` with `notifications.group_id` NULL and no group\nrow at all, and `:275-276` adds `notifications_target_ck CHECK (subject_kind = 'digest' OR group_id IS\nNOT NULL)` — one CHECK arm per conversation key. `internal/notification/service/notify.go:702`\n(`threadSubjectOf`) is already two-armed for the same reason. A rename would carry that seam forward\nunder a new spelling.\n\n**Why it matters.** Two things in `23303a0` were expensively derived and should not be re-derived by\nwhoever reads it next, so they are recorded here.\n\n*The naming panel's finding, for the record.* `notification_groups` was recommended, with\n`alert_conversations` as runner-up. If `7570090` is ever abandoned and the object survives, that is\nwhere the naming work restarts — and note that `notification_groups` was the recommendation on the\nassumption `23303a0` done-when 2 names: that the object remains the notification unit.\n\n*`23303a0` done-when 6 is factually wrong and would send an implementer to the wrong file.* It says\n*\"`test/scope/allocation_test.go` still enforces the allocation under the new name.\"* That file\ncontains **zero** occurrences of `alert_group`, `AlertGroup`, or even the bare substring `group` —\nverified with `git grep -c \"group\" -- test/scope/allocation_test.go`, which matches nothing. The arch\ntest that IS affected is `test/arch/sqltables_test.go`, at four sites: `:151` (the ownership row\n`table: \"alert_groups\", owner: \"grouping\", access: tableDelete`), `:176`, `:359` (`syntheticGuarded`)\nand `:536` (the retention list).\n\n**Where.** `23303a0` (the superseded ticket) · `7570090` (what supersedes it) ·\n`test/scope/allocation_test.go` (named in `23303a0` done-when 6, not affected) ·\n`test/arch/sqltables_test.go:151,176,359,536` (affected, and what done-when 6 should have said).\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** `23303a0` is closed as superseded by `7570090` — by the owner, not by an implementer —\nand nobody starts the rename. If `7570090` is declined instead, `23303a0` reopens with done-when 6\ncorrected to name `test/arch/sqltables_test.go` and with `notification_groups` already chosen, so the\nnaming panel is not convened a second time.","files":null}]}